    {
      "id": "sec-5",
      "title": "Energy in Electromagnetic Waves",
      "module": "m42446",
      "est_minutes": 12,
      "locator": "CNXML module m42446",
      "objectives": [
        "Explain how the energy and amplitude of an electromagnetic wave are related.",
        "Given its power output and the heating area, calculate the intensity of a microwave oven’s electromagnetic field, as well as its peak electric and magnetic field strengths"
      ],
      "misconceptions": [],
      "summary": [
        "The energy carried by any wave is proportional to its amplitude squared. For electromagnetic waves, this means intensity can be expressed as Iave=cε0E022,where Iave is the average intensity in W/m2, and E0 is the maximum electric field strength of a continuous sinusoidal wave.",
        "This can also be expressed in terms of the maximum magnetic field strength B0 as I ave = cB 0 2 2μ 0 and in terms of both electric and magnetic fields as Iave=E0B02μ0.",
        "The three expressions for Iave are all equivalent."
      ],
